WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.930
For eight seasons on Showtime, we thought
Dexter was one wrong move from getting caught

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.410
-- but he always got away.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.190
And now, the vigilante serial killer is coming
back for more.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:14.980
Pull on some latex gloves and dig into some
of the juiciest facts about Showtime's gruesome

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:15.980
hit series.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.040
From "Outlander" to "Game of Thrones" and
"The Vampire Diaries," plenty of hit TV shows

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.330
are based on popular series of books, and
"Dexter" is no different.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:30.619
But while those other examples are all extensions
of wildly bestselling books, the "Dexter"

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:35.120
series wasn't as well-known a title when it
was selected for adaptation.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.670
Executive producer Sara Colleton decided to
take a chance on developing the series when

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:45.010
she came across Jeff Lindsay's first Dexter-based
crime novel, "Darkly Dreaming Dexter."

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.920
Oddly, she hadn't even read the book before
she started working on getting it made into

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.170
a TV series.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.660
On an episode of Sundance Channel's "The Writer's
Room," Colleton revealed that she was simply

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.109
intrigued by a review of the book she read
in "The New Yorker" in 2004.

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:05.110
"It said it was a likable, vigilante serial
killer who had an 'ordinary Joe'-ness about

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.110
him.”

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:10.080
The first season of "Dexter" is inspired by
the novel, which similarly follows a Miami

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.310
blood spatter analyst named Dexter Morgan
who secretly murders horrible criminals after

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.610
being taught to do so by his ex-cop foster
father.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.880
But the show's writers used up most of the
material from the first book in the pilot

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.750
episode, meaning that the show and the source
material started veering away from each other

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.380
very early on.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.832
Lindsay ultimately wrote eight Dexter novels,
which only bear a passing resemblance to their

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:33.832
TV cousin.

00:01:34.832 --> 00:01:38.711
For Michael C. Hall, playing Dexter Morgan
just might be his signature role, the one

00:01:38.711 --> 00:01:41.140
for which he's best and most remembered.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.000
But before he started pretending to be a renegade
dealer of death, Hall had won attention — and

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.960
an Emmy Award nomination — for his role
as second-generation mortician David Fisher

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.350
on HBO's funeral home drama "Six Feet Under."

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.570
Hall said on an episode of Sundance Channel's
"The Writers' Room" that he saw the "Dexter"

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.700
pilot script just a few months after he finished
up his five-year run on "Six Feet Under."

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.240
He wasn't interested in another show, and
certainly not one thematically similar to

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.349
his previous one.

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.770
"The last thing I thought I'd do was another
television series."

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:18.400
"I didn't want to make another open-ended
commitment to a character surrounded by dead

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.400
bodies."

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.849
But after reading the script and novel it
was based off of, he ultimately decided that

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:24.939
it was too good of a part to pass up.

00:02:24.939 --> 00:02:29.180
Christian Camargo portrayed the primary villain
in the first season of "Dexter," a fellow

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.099
serial killer known as the Ice Truck Killer.

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:37.260
But for that part, co-executive producer Michael
Cuesta had a different guy in mind: Jeremy

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.709
Renner, at the time best known for playing
serial killer Jeffrey Dahmer in the 2002 movie

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:43.709
"Dahmer."

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:47.989
Renner later said in an interview with Cloture
Club that he rejected Cuesta's offer, saying,

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:51.700
"I can't do any other serial killer movies
or TV shows."

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.310
He almost took the role anyway, but had a
scheduling conflict with "28 Weeks Later."

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.159
Writers name their characters for lots of
different reasons.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:04.680
Sometimes they just like the name, it suits
a character, or just rolls well off the tongue.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.319
But other times, character names can hold
a multi-lingual web of intrigue full of secrets,

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.790
Easter eggs, and hidden meanings -- and that's
the case with Dexter.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:17.879
In Latin, "dexter" isn't generally a first
name but rather a word, and it means "right,"

00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:18.879
or "right-handed."

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:21.540
The Latin word for the left hand is "sinister."

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.099
Dexter certainly has a sinister side, what
with all the secret murders.

00:03:25.099 --> 00:03:29.670
He often refers to this side of himself as
the "Dark Passenger," the nickname he gives

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.310
to the side of his personality that drives
him to kill.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.629
Taking the metaphor all the way home, then,
"Dexter" would be the "normal" personality

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:39.260
that the character presents to others.

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:45.650
"Dexter" is a bloody, violent, and disturbing
TV show, and it fits right in on a premium

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:46.719
cable channel.

00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:50.700
And yet, for a brief period, it actually ran
on a broadcast network.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:57.080
In 2007 and 2008, the Writers Guild of America
went on strike for more than three months.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.060
With more than 60 scripted television series
shutting down production as a result of the

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:06.319
strike, the big networks were left with major
holes in their TV schedules.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:11.810
With relatively tame crime-and-cop shows like
"CSI" and "Criminal Minds" benched, CBS pulled

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.620
content from its corporate sibling, Showtime.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:20.290
All 12 episodes from the first season of "Dexter"
aired in a carefully edited form, which reportedly

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.910
marked the first time in TV history that a
broadcast channel aired a whole season of

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.500
a premium cable series.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.890
Two people in the cast of "Dexter" found love
amidst the show's dark premise: Michael C.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:37.000
Hall and Jennifer Carpenter, who played Dexter's
adoptive sister Debra Morgan.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.510
The general public didn't even know the two
had been a couple for more than a year and

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:45.380
a half until they showed up at the Golden
Globes in January 2009, having eloped and

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:47.840
gotten married just a few days prior.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.920
But the marriage wasn't meant to last.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.690
Just about three years later, Hall and Carpenter
released a statement to Entertainment Weekly

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.200
announcing that they had separated and were
filing for divorce.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.280
It was evidently an amicable enough split
that Hall and Carpenter continued to work

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.820
with each other on "Dexter" for a few more
years.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:10.130
Still, art managed to imitate life when "Dexter"
writers explored a romantic relationship between

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.620
the brother and sister.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:12.620
Awkward?

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.320
Well, probably -- but the actors handled it
like pros.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.540
Over the course of 96 episodes, Dexter Morgan
killed a lot of people.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.980
But how many lives did the character take
in total?

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:27.440
The authors of the "Dexter's Kill List" page
on a Dexter fan site carefully watched all

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.250
episodes and tallied the number of victims
by both counting up who he killed on screen

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.440
and looking at the number of blood slides
he kept in his box of souvenirs.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.890
All in all, Dexter had something to do with
the death of at least 168 people.

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:46.300
A lot of those killings occurred within the
time frame of 2006 to 2013, when "Dexter"

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.300
originally aired.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.830
If Dexter had been a real person, he would
have been one of the most prolific murderers

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.140
in the history of Miami, if not the whole
world.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.140
"Deb, I'm the Bay Harbor Butcher."

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.350
"On the ground.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.910
Down on the ground, motherf---er."

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:03.560
During the same period as the show, the real
Miami witnessed between 59 and 79 murders

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:04.560
annually.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:10.250
Proportionally, this would make Dexter single-handedly
responsible for 15 to 20 percent of all the

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.450
city's murders.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.970
Showtime's "Dexter" isn't the first screen
adaptation of a book about a handsome and

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:20.230
unflappable young man whose demeanor hides
a penchant for brutal murder.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:26.590
In 1991, Bret Easton Ellis published "American
Psycho," his cult classic that satirized materialistic

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:32.460
'80s culture and centered around an investment
banker and probable serial killer named Patrick

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.470
Bateman.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:37.290
In 2000, Christian Bale portrayed Bateman
in the film version of "American Psycho,"

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.250
and references to the character and book he
appears in have popped up throughout "Dexter."

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:47.030
Dexter uses several aliases in his criminal
activities, and he buys animal tranquilizers

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.650
under the name "Patrick Bateman, M.D."

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:54.530
When he schedules an appointment with a therapist
he's investigating, Dexter says his name is

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:55.530
"Sean Ellis."

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.720
That's another "American Psycho" reference.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.080
In the book, Patrick Bateman's brother is
named Sean, and "Ellis" is obviously the author

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.210
of the book.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.900
Despite the loving tributes, Ellis apparently
isn't a "Dexter" fan.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.470
In 2012, he tweeted a blunt review of the
series, writing,

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.010
"'Dexter' sucks big time.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:17.130
A high-concept cutesy serial-killer drama
that has as much depth and scares as an episode

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.660
of 'American Psycho 2.'"

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:24.110
In the midst of filming the fourth season
of "Dexter" in 2009, star Michael C. Hall

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:29.680
received some devastating news: Doctors diagnosed
him with Hodgkin's lymphoma, a type of cancer

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.180
that attacks the immune system.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.680
Hall didn't take any time off of work, nor
did he announce his situation to the "Dexter"

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.680
cast or crew.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.880
Instead, he kept up with the rigors of filming
the Showtime drama, and then received his

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.580
first chemotherapy treatment for cancer the
day after filming had wrapped for the season.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:50.160
In 2014, after the series' initial run had
wrapped, Hall spoke about his thinking at

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.080
the time in an interview with The Guardian,
saying,

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.020
"I thought, 'Well, I can treat this successfully
and if my hair falls out I'll wear a wig during

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.710
the fifth season — and I won't even have
to share with anyone that this is happening.'"

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:05.600
Hall's hair did fall out, and when he attended
the 2010 Golden Globes and Screen Actors Guild

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.140
Awards, he did so with a knit cap on his head.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:13.030
He didn't initially intend to reveal his diagnosis
to the public, but when he did, he didn't

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:14.030
regret it.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:15.030
He told The Guardian,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.170
"I'm glad I did, because I really underestimated
how much that would be a source of inspiration

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.960
or strength to other people.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.480
Just to see someone who you know and spend
time watching, who goes through the treatment

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:26.650
process successfully, is of value."

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:31.491
Michael C. Hall prepared for his role of Dexter
by doing something right out of the Dexter

00:08:31.491 --> 00:08:35.449
playbook -- identifying, and stalking, his
potential victims.

00:08:35.449 --> 00:08:39.680
Before shooting on the series began, Hall
did some traditional preparation for the role,

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:42.829
watching documentaries and reading books about
serial killers.

00:08:42.829 --> 00:08:46.699
But he still felt like he needed to feel what
being a stalking predator searching for their

00:08:46.699 --> 00:08:48.120
prey might be like.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.279
Hall said on "George Stroumboulopoulos Tonight,"

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:54.779
"I would go out by myself in New York and
try to find someone who was also alone, maybe

00:08:54.779 --> 00:08:56.600
go to a restaurant with a bar.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.839
And I would watch them finish their meal and
endow them with all kinds of reprehensible

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:03.630
characteristics, a potential victim for Dexter."

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.220
"I wasn't going to actually do anything.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.379
I would see what it felt like to follow them
a little bit."

00:09:09.379 --> 00:09:13.540
Then Hall would quietly but intensely follow
his target through the streets of New York,

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:17.749
seemingly doing everything a real killer would
do except, well, really killing.

00:09:17.749 --> 00:09:19.810
"I was raised with a certain set of principles."

00:09:22.829 --> 00:09:27.800
In a tragic case of life imitating art, the
fictional murders committed by Dexter Morgan

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.310
may have led to real-life killings.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.649
In 2009, Indiana teenager Andrew Conley turned
himself in to police and admitted strangling

00:09:35.649 --> 00:09:37.040
his brother to death.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.310
He claimed to have thought about killing for
years, and felt a kinship with Dexter Morgan

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.170
for feeling compelled to do so.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:48.900
In 2014, an English teenager reportedly very
into "Dexter" stabbed his girlfriend to death,

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.589
then disposed of the body with tree-trimming
tools.

00:09:51.589 --> 00:09:55.820
He soon thereafter confessed to his sister
that his own "Dark Passenger" had compelled

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:56.870
him to kill.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:02.899
There's also Mark Twitchell, a "Dexter" devotee
and aspiring filmmaker who made a "Dexter"-esque

00:10:02.899 --> 00:10:06.130
fan film and posted as "Dexter Morgan" on
social media.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:11.499
In 2011, Twitchell turned a film studio in
his garage into his own elaborate "kill room"

00:10:11.499 --> 00:10:13.990
and lured an innocent man to his violent death.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:17.029
He was caught promptly, and convicted of first-degree
murder.

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:22.689
In 2013, "Dexter" ended its original run with
a much-anticipated series finale.

00:10:22.689 --> 00:10:25.220
Would his crimes finally catch up to him?

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:26.220
Not exactly.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:31.220
Instead, Dexter abandoned his only child,
buried his sister at sea, faked his death,

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:36.620
and started life over under an assumed identity
as a lumberjack in the Pacific Northwest.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:37.830
"That's disappointing."

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:43.660
It wasn't just fans who wanted Dexter to face
some kind of consequences after eight years

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.180
of getting away with it.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.790
Many members of the series' creative team
wanted more closure with the ending as well.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.639
After the season eight finale in 2013, "Dexter"
producer John Goldwyn told Vulture,

00:10:54.639 --> 00:10:56.680
"They won't let us kill him.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:57.949
Showtime was very clear about that.

00:10:57.949 --> 00:11:01.990
When we told them the arc for the last season,
they just said, 'Just to be clear, he's going

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:02.990
to live.'"

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:06.899
In an interview with E! News, former "Dexter"
showrunner Clyde Phillips revealed what he

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:10.410
would have done if he'd been in charge of
crafting the show's final scene.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.410
He said,

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.040
"In the very last scene of the series.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.129
Dexter wakes up.

00:11:14.129 --> 00:11:18.110
[...] Dexter's opening his eyes and he's on
the execution table at the Florida Penitentiary.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:22.129
They're just starting to administer the drugs
and he looks out through the window to the

00:11:22.129 --> 00:11:23.519
observation gallery.

00:11:23.519 --> 00:11:26.019
And in the gallery are all the people that
Dexter killed."

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:30.230
As Phillips conceived it, the observers would
have been everyone who ever died as a result

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:34.839
of Dexter's actions, from the Trinity Killer
to LaGuerta and Doakes to his minor kills

00:11:34.839 --> 00:11:35.839
of the week.

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:38.249
A lot of people didn't like the way "Dexter"
wrapped up.

00:11:38.249 --> 00:11:42.100
No matter how you feel about it, it's hard
to argue that this would have been one hell

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:43.189
of a way to go.

00:11:44.189 --> 00:11:47.760
The silver lining of keeping Dexter alive
was always that the show could one day see

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:51.600
a revival, and get a second chance to wrap
things up in a satisfying way.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.529
Now, that do-over is finally coming to pass.

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:59.990
In 2020, seven years after "Dexter" aired
its last episode, Showtime ordered 10 new

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.579
episodes, to be presented as a limited series.

00:12:02.579 --> 00:12:07.069
Michael C. Hall signed on to once again play
Dexter, while the series' original executive

00:12:07.069 --> 00:12:10.610
producer Clyde Phillips agreed to serve as
showrunner.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:15.300
In April 2021, Showtime unveiled a teaser
trailer for the revival and announced that

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:16.899
it would air the following fall.

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:21.610
Plot details have initially been scarce, but
reports indicate that the episodes will find

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:26.269
Dexter Morgan hiding out under the assumed
identity of Jim Lindsay in the small town

00:12:26.269 --> 00:12:27.610
of Iron Lake, New York.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:32.360
There, a mystery will ensnare the mayor, the
chief of police, and a true-crime podcast

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:36.670
host -- as well as Dexter Morgan, back from
the dead to kill again.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.939
"Dexter's dark passenger is still with him."

00:12:38.939 --> 00:12:41.119
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:41.119 --> 00:12:45.869
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies and TV shows are coming soon.

